ation"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/748"} "Sydney Exhibition Centre, Sydney Convention and Exhibition Centre, Sydney, New South Wales"] " (Hall 1 &amp; 2)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/18380"} "34 from 34 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two single-elimination pools, with winner of each pool advanced to the final. All judoka losing to the finalists in each pool advanced to repêchage pools, with the winners of the repêchage pools earning bronze medals."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "After winning the 1996 gold medal in this class, " [:a {:href "/athletes/34155"} "Tadahiro Nomura"] " also won the 1997 World Championships. He did not compete at the 1999 World Championships, won by Cuba’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/34243"} "Manolo Poulot"] ". Both were drawn in Pool B at Sydney and met in the pool final, after they had easily won their preliminary matches. It was an epic match with Nomura winning only by " [:em {} "yusei-gachi"] ". After that match, the final was easy for Nomura. Facing Korea’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/92360"} "Jeong Seok-Gyeong"] ". Nomura won with " [:em {} "sumi-otoshi"] " for " [:em {} "ippon"] " in only 14 seconds, the fastest final in Olympic history. Nomura would win his third consecutive gold medal in this event in 2004. He then stayed out of competition for two years but returned to try to win a four gold medal in 2008. However, he did not make the Japanese team for Beijing and retired in mid-2008."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/34155"} "Tadahiro Nomura"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {